leon panetta has served as white house  chief of staff head of the cia and the  u.s defense secretary he told us that  the french should really be more  supportive more on that in a moment but  first i began by asking him for his  reaction to this week's admission that  one of america's last acts in  afghanistan a drone strike in kabul had  killed 10 innocent people  it's not good news uh i look uh  drone strikes or weapons of war  and you have to  exercise tremendous  caution  when you're targeting  with a drone  as someone who's been involved involved  with drone operations as  director of the cia  i thought it was very important  to have  intelligence not only  using our surveillance capability but  also  using people on the ground spies on the  ground  to identify those targets and make sure  that  we had the right targets when we fired  in this instance it's pretty clear that  that kind of care was not exercised  and a terrible mistake has been made  and it does reflect badly i think on the  the approach  that was used to decide what target to  hit i know there was a tremendous amount  of confusion i know there was a  tremendous amount of pressure  to try to go after those that had killed  our marines  and killed others  but that's no excuse  for not developing accurate information  when it comes to targeting individuals  do you think that this kind of mistake  let's call it that  weakens the united states hand in its  foreign policy and its work to try to  extend its influence across the globe  well look at any anytime  this kind of mistake is made  uh it uh it reflects badly  uh in terms of  the mythology  of  applying  our technology to  targets on the ground  now you know having said that  we live in a very dangerous world  uh and we're dealing with a lot of  adversaries  whether it's russia whether it's china  whether it's iran whether it's north  korea  whether it's failed states in the middle  east whether it's terrorism  and  the fact is that we still are at war  with terrorism  i think the result in afghanistan is  that  we have a taliban  that will probably continue to provide  safe haven  to terrorists like al qaeda and isis and  haqqanis and others  and so we're going to have to  make sure  that in dealing with that threat to our  national security  uh yes we're going to need the very best  intelligence we can get on those targets  to the extent that a mistake was made  here  yes  it's something that  we have to worry about we have to  investigate as to what happened  and we have to make sure that it never  happens again  can i turn now to  wider issues of foreign policy  is it fair to say that the biden  administration's  foreign policy has just one overwhelming  priority china  well there's no question that uh  china is a priority  but  i i'm not sure you can call it  the only overwhelming priority we have  we're probably facing more flash points  in the world than we face since the end  of world war ii  because it isn't just china  it's also russia which has become much  more aggressive  in their actions particularly towards  the united states  iran is continuing  to enrich fuel  and is getting closer to the capability  of having a nuclear weapon  north korea is now firing more missiles  and developing more nuclear weapons  and we are continuing to see terrorism  and we're continuing to see cyber  attacks which is the battlefield of the  future  so we're dealing with a lot of threats  and yes china is one of those  but the united states has to be capable  of being able to respond  to national security threats across the  board  and that means that we have to be strong  enough militarily and diplomatically to  be able to deal with those challenges  when the do you were  the  chief of staff in the white house um  your administration was pretty clear on  china it  thought that the globalization and  economic growth would lead to greater  freedom  and a different relationship with china  and reform in china itself  it hasn't worked out that way has it  unfortunately uh it has not worked out  that way  and there's been a lot of mistakes along  the way  i think president obama was right when  he talked about rebalancing our force to  the pacific i was secretary of defense  when we made that decision  and it was important to do that  uh in order to make clear to china that  yes we will work with them we will have  dialogue with them we'll try to deal  with them economically and financially  as as we have  but at the same time  we are also going to protect our  national interests  when it comes to the pacific which means  that the south china sea  cannot  be an area  that limits the ability of shipping  and free trade  and freedom of the seas to be able to  take place  it means that we have to respect human  rights  it means that we have to protect uh  taiwan  it means that we have to make sure that  the right thing is done with regards to  hong kong as well  so  yes we we can have that dialogue  but at the same time i think we have to  be prepared  in order to show china  that there are lines that they cannot  cross  is the pact signed this week between the  united states uh the united kingdom and  australia to provide australia with  nuclear-powered submarines  uh  a preparation as you've just  talked about or is it as the chinese are  saying a provocation  i think it's about engagement and i  think it's about deterrence  i think that's that's the message that  they should get uh  you know i  i think that the  the most important thing  that needs to be done  uh in dealing with china is to  strengthen our alliances the one thing  the chinese don't do very well is have  allies  that's not something that works well for  them same thing is true for russia  they don't like other countries  developing alliances that might limit  them  in the decisions they make  and so i think the fact that uh  we announced this security partnership  and that we made clear  that we are going to b